/*
  Geometry for the "figma" signature (Figma frame 1:155), generated offline
  (scratch: signature-variants/figma/work, scripts/emit-figma.mjs). Frame space: 1512 × 982 design px.

  Script: "ag impact partners" in WindSong by Robert Leuschke (github.com/googlefonts/windsong), SIL
  Open Font License 1.1: the closest OFL match to the Figma's Maddison Signature (thin, very slanted,
  wide, low x-height, long t-crosses). Glyphs a g i m p a c t.002 p a r t.002 n e r s taken as outlines
  with opentype.js at 126 px (font kerning, the alternate t with the long cross), centred at x 853.5
  on a baseline at y 570, 1052 px wide like the Figma text.

  Pen route: derived from the outlines, not drawn by hand. Each glyph was rasterised, thinned to a
  skeleton (Zhang–Suen), turned into a graph and walked in writing order (letters left to right, the
  i dotted and each t crossed right after their stem, the p stems retraced, joins kept in contact).
  Each stroke is smoothed (cusps kept sharp) and split into chunks of near-constant width (the local
  ink thickness plus a margin), so a mask of round-capped strokes uncovers the ink exactly where the
  pen passes. Coverage checked: the masks uncover every pixel of the ink.

  Lines: the two Figma vectors (3:176 lead-in, 3:183 outro) were drawn with a mouse. They were
  resampled, lightly smoothed and refitted with few cubic Béziers with continuous tangents (p95
  deviation from the Figma under 2.5 px), then re-attached to the script: the lead-in now runs up the
  first a's stem into its first stroke, the outro leaves the s's exit stroke.

  FIGMA_INK: the script's ink, one filled outline per mask group (letters that flow into each other
  share a group: a · g · i · m · p · a · c · t · p · ar · t · ners).
  FIGMA_PEN: strokes in writing order. `entry`: "lead" continues the lead-in, "join" keeps contact
  (the pen moves on at writing speed), "lift" is a pen lift; `air` is the distance from the previous
  stroke's end. Each chunk is [path, mask width, time weight]: its length, a touch more in tight turns.
  FIGMA_LEAD / FIGMA_OUTRO: cubic Bézier chains [x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3, x4, y4, …]. When the
  measured headline differs from the Figma's (French copy, taller viewport), each line bends: near
  its headline end it follows the anchor, near the script it stays with the script, and in between
  (`blend`, a stretch of its length from 0 to 1) the pull eases from one to the other.
*/

